  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>The school safety audit is a routine walk covering buildings, grounds, fire, services and stored hazards, sitting under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974</li><li>The school is the employer and duty holder; the DfE's Good Estate Management for Schools sets the expected standard</li><li>The audit does not replace specialist assessments: fire risk assessment, asbestos survey, legionella risk assessment and the annual playground inspection each need a competent person</li><li>The value of the audit is the record and the action that follows a finding, not the tick itself</li><li>Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, and dormant users are free, so a trust can add every caretaker, site manager and central team member and only pay for who actually works</li></ul></div><h2>What the school safety audit is for</h2><p>A school health and safety audit is a routine, structured walk of the site that checks the everyday hazards a school carries: trip and fall risks on paths and floors, obstructed fire exits, faulty electrical fittings, unsecured chemicals, damaged fencing, and the condition of known asbestos-containing materials in older buildings. It is the general safety net that runs between the deeper, specialist assessments.</p><p>The audit does not make the school safe. Acting on what it finds does. A checklist that gets ticked and filed changes nothing; a finding that gets raised, assigned and closed out is the whole point. The tool's job is to make the walk easy to complete honestly on site, and to make the finding impossible to lose. This is general guidance, not legal advice; check the current DfE and HSE guidance for your setting.</p><h2>The main areas of a school safety walk</h2><h3>1. Buildings and grounds</h3><p>Paths, steps, handrails, floor surfaces, glazing, fencing and gates, checked for trip, fall and access hazards across the whole site, indoors and out.</p><h3>2. Fire and escape</h3><p>Fire doors, extinguishers, alarm call points, emergency lighting, signage and clear escape routes, under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005. The audit checks routine condition; the fire risk assessment is separate.</p><h3>3. Services and stored hazards</h3><p>Electrical safety, water systems, and chemicals stored under the Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations, plus the condition of known asbestos-containing materials in older buildings under the Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012.</p><h2>Who holds the duty</h2><p>The Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 places the primary duty on the employer to protect staff, pupils and visitors. For a maintained school the employer is usually the local authority or the governing body; for an academy it is the trust. The site manager and caretaker carry out the audit, but the legal responsibility for a safe estate sits with the responsible body, which is why the record has to be as reliable as the walk. The DfE's Good Estate Management for Schools sets the expected standard, and a school is expected to have access to competent health and safety advice.</p><h2>What still needs a specialist</h2><p>The safety audit is deliberately broad and shallow. It flags that a fire door is wedged open, but it is not the fire risk assessment required under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005. It notes damaged material in a known asbestos location, but it is not an asbestos survey. It checks that water outlets run as expected, but it is not the legionella risk assessment. Each is a distinct duty needing a competent person. Safeguarding under Keeping Children Safe in Education is a separate matter again, distinct from premises safety.</p><h2>What to look for in a school audit tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to complete the walk as it happens, not afterwards at a desk</li><li><strong>Findings that become actions:</strong> a hazard raised should turn into an assigned, trackable action, not a note that vanishes</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of observation:</strong> each finding tied to a timestamped image</li><li><strong>Trust-wide visibility:</strong> a central dashboard across every site, so the responsible body can see overdue audits and open actions</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free, unlimited users, so adding staff and sites carries no licence penalty</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the audit and its findings and gives the trust a live view across sites. It does not make a school safe, provide competent health and safety advice, or replace a fire risk assessment, asbestos survey or legionella risk assessment. Those remain the responsible body's to commission from a competent person. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your school or trust.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What should a school health and safety audit cover?</h3><p>A school safety audit should cover buildings and grounds (paths, steps, surfaces, glazing, fencing), fire safety (doors, alarms, extinguishers, escape routes), services (electrical and water safety), stored hazards under COSHH, and the condition of known asbestos-containing materials in older buildings. It is a routine walk under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974, distinct from the specialist assessments each of those areas may also require.</p><h3>Who is responsible for health and safety in a school?</h3><p>The employer is the duty holder under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974. For a maintained school that is usually the local authority or governing body, and for an academy it is the trust. Staff carry out the audit, but the legal duty for a safe estate sits with the responsible body. The DfE's Good Estate Management for Schools sets the expected standard.</p><h3>Does the audit replace a fire risk assessment or asbestos survey?</h3><p>No. The safety audit is a broad routine walk. It does not replace the fire risk assessment required under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005, the asbestos survey under the Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012, or a legionella risk assessment. Each of those is a distinct duty needing a competent person. The audit flags issues in those areas; the specialist assessment addresses them.</p><h3>Can I run a school safety audit over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's audit workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The site manager receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed audit report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the school's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/school-premises-inspection-software-uk.html\">School premises inspection software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/playground-equipment-inspection-uk.html\">Playground equipment inspection</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/legionella-water-check-schools-uk.html\">Legionella water checks for schools</a></li></ul>",
